  "title": "Cory G NDE 6280",
  "date": "6/1978",
  "content": "At the time of this event, I was employed by a large regional FM rock-formatted radio station as an air personality. To promote the station and earn extra money, I regularly accepted paid appearances, which were promoted on-air to raise awareness of an event and raise money for the organization. This happened at one of those events and I would guess a couple of hundred people were in attendance. My fellow air personalities had been giving me a hard time, prior to the event, because, despite the radio station's location at the beach, I could not swim and the event was being held to raise funds to improve the community's swimming pool. The idea was raised on-air that it would be hilarious if somehow I 'ended up' in the pool. When I arrived on scene, I gathered the lifeguards and told them that if they had any ideas in this regard they should know that I actually could not swim. Although they might see me voluntarily get in the pool, I would be staying in the shallow end. In the event they threw me in, they should be prepared to immediately get me out as because I could not swim. Toward the end of the day, I was grabbed by about four large lifeguards, thrown in the pool, and proceeded to lose all sense of direction. It seemed that the pool was built with a ledge, which protruded over the water, and somehow, in flailing about, I was stuck in that area. In addition, my eyes are very sensitive and I could not clearly see. I thrashed about trying to surface, which was impossible given my position. My muscles lost all strength, my lungs filled with water and I stopped breathing. It was extremely painful and I realized my life was finished. Meantime, the lifeguards who threw me in thought I was playing a joke on them and could really swim. Boy were they wrong!",
